  </section>

  <!-- Article Content -->
  <article class="py-16 px-4">
    <div class="container mx-auto max-w-3xl prose prose-lg">
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8 font-light">
        Week two of From Scratch Friday was a bit more ambitious. We're talking cookies, spinach and artichoke dip with toasted baguette slices, and homemade blueberry lemonade. Leo helped out where he could, and Raphael supervised from his bouncer (as any good 3-month-old executive chef would).
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-12 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">Blueberry Lemonade (The Star of the Show)</h2>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        We started with the lemonade because honestly, fresh-squeezed lemonade with real blueberries? That's the kind of thing that makes a Friday feel special.
      </p>

      <!-- Lemons and Blueberries Image -->
      <div class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm">
        <img src="/images/lemons-and-blueberries-in-bowls-ready-for-prep.png" alt="Fresh lemons and blueberries in bowls ready for prep" class="w-full" />
        <div class="p-4 bg-soho-white">
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 font-light">Fresh lemons and blueberries prepped and ready to go</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Leo helped juice the lemons (with lots of enthusiasm and a little bit of chaos). We made a simple syrup on the stove—just equal parts sugar and water, heated until the sugar dissolves.
      </p>

      <!-- Pot of Sugar Image -->
      <div class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm">
        <img src="/images/pot-of-sugar.png" alt="Pot of sugar on the stove for simple syrup" class="w-full" />
        <div class="p-4 bg-soho-white">
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 font-light">Making simple syrup on the stove</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Then we muddled fresh blueberries into the lemon juice, added the simple syrup and cold water, and boom—blueberry lemonade that actually tastes like the fruit came from nature, not a lab.
      </p>

      <!-- Blueberry Lemonade Mix Image -->
      <div class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm">
        <img src="/images/blueberry-lemonade-mix.png" alt="Fresh blueberry lemonade mixture with muddled blueberries" class="w-full" />
        <div class="p-4 bg-soho-white">
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 font-light">The finished blueberry lemonade with muddled blueberries</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Leo with Lemonade Image -->
      <div class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm">
        <img src="/images/leo-and-a-cup-of-his-lemonade.png" alt="Leo enjoying a cup of his homemade blueberry lemonade" class="w-full" />
        <div class="p-4 bg-soho-white">
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 font-light">Leo proudly enjoying his blueberry lemonade creation</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-12 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">Spinach and Artichoke Dip with Toasted Baguette</h2>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        This is one of those recipes that sounds fancy but is actually pretty straightforward. Cream cheese, sour cream, mozzarella, parmesan, spinach, and artichoke hearts all mixed together and baked until bubbly and golden.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        The key is getting the spinach squeezed dry (nobody wants watery dip) and chopping the artichoke hearts small enough that they blend in nicely.
      </p>

      <!-- Prepped Baguette Image -->
      <div class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm">
        <img src="/images/prepped-baguette.png" alt="Sliced baguette ready for toasting" class="w-full" />
        <div class="p-4 bg-soho-white">
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 font-light">Fresh baguette sliced and ready for toasting</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        We sliced up a fresh baguette, brushed the pieces with olive oil, and toasted them in the oven until they were crispy and golden. Perfect for scooping up that creamy, cheesy dip.
      </p>

      <!-- Final Dip Image -->
      <div class="my-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 shadow-sm">
        <img src="/images/spinach-and-artichoke-heart-dip-with-toasted-baguette.png" alt="Spinach and artichoke dip with toasted baguette slices" class="w-full" />
        <div class="p-4 bg-soho-white">
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 font-light">The finished spinach and artichoke dip with toasted baguette</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-12 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">The Cookies</h2>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Full transparency: we made cookies from scratch, but I'm writing this blog post at 11 PM and I'm too tired to remember the exact recipe we used. Classic chocolate chip, butter, sugar, eggs, flour—you know the drill.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Leo helped mix the dough and (mostly) didn't eat too many chocolate chips before they made it into the cookies. We're calling that a win.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-bold mt-12 mb-6 text-gray-900 tracking-tight">What We Learned This Week</h2>

      <ul class="space-y-3 text-gray-600 mb-6">
        <li><strong class="text-gray-900">Blueberry lemonade is worth the effort.</strong> Store-bought lemonade just doesn't compare to fresh-squeezed with muddled blueberries.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-gray-900">Toasted baguette beats chips every time.</strong> The crunch, the flavor, the way it holds up to dip—just better.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-gray-900">Three-year-olds are excellent taste-testers.</strong> If Leo approves, it's good.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-gray-900">From Scratch Friday is becoming a thing.</strong> Even when we're tired, it's worth it to make something together as a family.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="bg-mcgennis-gold bg-opacity-10 border-l-4 border-mcgennis-gold p-6 my-8 rounded-r-lg">
        <p class="text-gray-700 font-medium mb-2">Next Friday:</p>
        <p class="text-gray-600">
          We'll be traveling, so no From Scratch Friday next week. But when we get back? We're thinking pizza from scratch. Dough, sauce, toppings—the whole thing. Follow along to see if we actually pull it off.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </article>

